      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 American Congress of Rehabilitation Medicine. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To describe the prevalence of musculoskeletal conditions (MSKC) in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD); to examine the sociodemographic, clinical, and psychosocial predictors of these comorbidities; and to describe health care utilization by musculoskeletal comorbidity status."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "This was a cross-sectional, observational study in which patients were administered a questionnaire in the hospital and 1 year later."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Eleven hospitals in Ontario, Canada."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "CAD patients (N=1803)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Not applicable."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Sociodemographic, MSKC, clinical, and psychosocial factors were ascertained via questionnaire and in-hospital chart extraction. A health care utilization questionnaire was mailed 1 year later."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Over half (56%) of the patients with CAD had MSKCs, with arthritis/joint pain accounting for 64.4% of these MSKCs. Patients who were older (odds ratio [OR]=1.03), women (OR=1.87), white (OR=1.80), with higher body mass index (OR=1.05), depressive symptoms (OR=1.92), and lower family income (OR=1.46) were more likely to present with MSKCs. One year posthospitalization, a greater proportion of those with MSKCs reported ≥1 cardiac-related emergency department visit (33.2% vs 28.3%, P=.03), hospital admission (30.7% vs 22%, P=.006), more primary care physician visits (6.6±5.6 vs 5.7±4.6, P<.001), and fewer cardiac rehabilitation referrals (61.5% vs 70%, P<.001). After adjusting for depressive symptoms, body mass index, age, income, ethnicity, and sex, MSKCs predicted only hospital readmissions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Over half of the patients hospitalized for CAD have MSKCs. Those with MSKCs have a physical and psychosocial profile that places them at greater cardiovascular risk than those with CAD only, explaining, in part, their greater health care utilization. Despite a greater need for comprehensive risk factor management in patients with MSKCs, fewer were referred to cardiac rehabilitation."
          }
        ]
      },
